<script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://lol.erik-schuetze.dev/about/","name":"About the data - how these statistics are produced - LoL Stats","description":"Where the numbers on this site will come from, how they are produced, the sample-size threshold below which a rate is withheld, the snapshot limitation on rank attribution, and what this site is not. This build publishes no Riot match data.","inLanguage":"en","isPartOf":{"@type":"WebSite","name":"LoL Stats","url":"/"}}]</script><article><h1>About this site and its data</h1><p>LoL Stats publishes ranked League of Legends statistics as static pages. The pages are rendered at build time from aggregate artifacts, in the same shape a real snapshot has, but these artifacts are illustrative demo data: no ingestion and aggregation pipeline has run for them, and nothing on this build is a measurement of a real game. Nothing on this site calls Riot's API while you are reading it, nothing here queries a database, and no page needs JavaScript to show its content.</p><h2>What this site is</h2><ul><li><strong>A ladder statistics reference.</strong> Win rate, pick rate and ban rate per champion and role, and champion-versus-champion matchup rates, for one patch, region, queue and rank bracket at a time.</li><li><strong>Static.</strong> Every page is a file that was rendered at build time. There is no request-time database, no session, no account and no API call behind a page load.</li><li><strong>Honest about its sample.</strong> Every rate is printed next to the number of games it was measured over, together with a 95% confidence half-width, and a cell below the publication threshold is withheld rather than printed.</li><li><strong>Free and ungated.</strong> Every page on this site is free and ungated: there is no account, no login, no paywall and no rate-limited teaser, and none is planned.</li><li><strong>Readable without JavaScript.</strong> Every route renders its primary content in the served HTML; any JavaScript in this site is optional.</li></ul><h2>What this site is not</h2><ul><li><strong>Not official, and not endorsed by Riot Games.</strong> This site is not affiliated with Riot Games, Inc., is not authorised, sponsored or approved by Riot Games, and is not an official source of League of Legends statistics.</li><li><strong>Not a rating service.</strong> This site does not compute, store or display an MMR, ELO or any other skill rating, and does not offer a calculator for one.</li><li><strong>Not a player lookup, a match-history viewer or a live-game tracker.</strong> There are no player pages and no per-player record of any kind in what this site publishes: no account, no summoner name, no PUUID, no individual match history.</li><li><strong>Not a data broker.</strong> This site publishes derived aggregate statistics only. It does not resell Riot data, does not serve the raw Riot API responses behind its aggregates, and publishes no per-player record, account, summoner name or match history.</li><li><strong>Not commercial.</strong> There is no advertising, no sponsorship, no subscription and no paid tier, and the site is not monetised in any way. It is a hobby project run at the operator's own expense.</li><li><strong>Not a betting or coaching product.</strong> Nothing here is a prediction, a recommendation or advice; see the <a href="/disclaimer">Disclaimer</a>.</li></ul><h2>What state this build is in</h2><p>This build publishes no Riot match data: the aggregate manifest this build read declares its source as &quot;demo&quot;, so every number it shows is illustrative preview data generated to exercise the layout. Nothing on this build is a measurement of a real game.</p><p>PREVIEW - illustrative data generated to exercise the layout, not real match statistics</p><ul><li>Patch <strong>16.18</strong> &middot; region EUW &middot; queue 420 &middot; rank bracket all</li><li>Crawl window: 2026-09-08 to 2026-09-14 (the window of match timestamps the aggregation covered, not the time it ran)</li><li>Snapshot generated 2026-09-15 04:10 UTC (the time the aggregate build ran)</li><li>Aggregated cells published: 141 across 80champions; cells withheld for being below the sample threshold: 3</li><li>Publication threshold: a win, pick or ban rate is published only for a cell holding at least n = 500 games</li><li>Build run 2 from commit 000000000000</li></ul><p>Two compliance requirements are still open rather than met, and this page says so plainly instead of implying otherwise. Riot's <em>verified site</em> requirement: The verified-site requirement is not met yet: this build was not given Riot&#39;s site-verification token, so https://lol.erik-schuetze.dev/riot.txt is not published and Riot has not verified this site. The token is issued to the domain owner, not to this repository, so the requirement is met only by a build that is given it. And the Riot API credential itself: this build was produced without a Riot production API key, so no match data has been ingested or published, and the site carries nothing that Riot has reviewed or approved. See <a href="/disclaimer">the Disclaimer</a> for the notices that stay visible until that changes.</p><h2>How the data will be produced</h2><p>Five steps, in order. No run of this pipeline has produced anything for this build - the aggregate manifest this build read declares its source as &quot;demo&quot; - so this is the method the numbers will come from, not a description of an ingestion that has happened. The pipeline is deliberately boring, because every interesting shortcut here would be a way to publish a wrong number.</p><ol><li><strong>Fetch.</strong> A long-running worker (<code>lolstats-ingest</code>) copies match records from Riot&#39;s ranked match API at an adaptive rate, and writes each response into an append-only raw archive as compressed Parquet, partitioned by API and date (<code>raw/riot/&lt;api&gt;/v&lt;n&gt;/dt=YYYY-MM-DD/</code>). Raw payloads are never rewritten in place: if the archive is wrong, the fix is a new partition, not an edit.</li><li><strong>Record.</strong> A PostgreSQL control plane tracks the crawl frontier, the fetch queue, deduplication keyed by Riot's <code>matchId</code>, and one row per build. It holds crawl state, not visitor state: the website never queries it, and it contains no user data of any kind.</li><li><strong>Aggregate.</strong> A nightly DuckDB build step reads the raw archive for the target patch window and computes counts per cell - patch, region, queue, bracket, champion, role, matchup pair. Cells below the publication threshold are suppressed and counted, never rounded up. The build writes<code>agg/v1/**</code> plus a manifest that records <code>n</code> for every published cell.</li><li><strong>Publish.</strong> The artifact tree is replaced atomically, so a reader sees either the old snapshot or the new one and never a half-written tree.</li><li><strong>Render.</strong> This site is built from those artifacts, with Riot's Data Dragon static data (names, numeric ids, icon paths) fetched at build time by <code>web/scripts/fetch-ddragon.mjs</code> and checked in, so that rendering a page needs no network access. The pages and the artifacts are served from the same origin, at<code>/</code> and <code>/agg/</code>.</li></ol><p>None of the five steps has run for this build: the aggregate manifest this build read declares its source as &quot;demo&quot;, so these pages were rendered from illustrative fixtures rather than from pipeline output.</p><h2>The sample size story</h2><p>Every rate on this site is printed next to the number of games it was measured over, and each rate carries a 95% confidence half-width. A 52% win rate over 900 games and a 52% win rate over 90,000 games are not the same claim, and the site is built so that you can tell them apart without doing arithmetic.</p><p>Where a cell is thinner than the publication threshold, this site does not print a rate at all. The cell reads "withheld" and shows the count it was withheld for. That is a deliberate refusal: a rate over a few dozen games is noise presented as a fact, and rounding it is not publishing it honestly. Cells that the aggregation suppressed before publication are counted in the notices on each page rather than silently dropped.</p><h2>How the numbers will be computed</h2><ul><li><strong>Source.</strong> Nothing yet: this build holds no match records, so nothing on it is computed from a real game. A real snapshot&#39;s source is Riot&#39;s ranked match records for ranked solo queue (queue 420), which the fetch step copies into a raw archive that is never rewritten in place.</li><li><strong>Grouping.</strong> Matches are grouped by patch, region, queue and rank bracket, then by champion and role, then by champion pair for matchups.</li><li><strong>Win rate</strong> is wins divided by games played in that champion-and-role cell. Games, not players: the same player twice counts twice.</li><li><strong>Pick rate</strong> is how often the champion was chosen for the role; <strong>ban rate</strong> is how often it was unavailable. Both are shares of the games in the window.</li><li><strong>Confidence intervals</strong> are a normal approximation of the binomial interval for the cell's sample, reported as a half-width in percentage points. They describe sampling error only; they say nothing about selection effects.</li><li><strong>Matchup pairs</strong> are stored once per unordered pair. The page shows both directions by taking the complement of the stored rate, which is arithmetic rather than a second sample.</li><li><strong>Tier letters</strong> are a derived reading aid computed from win rate and pick rate together. They are not an official ranking and Riot does not publish one.</li><li><strong>Champion identity</strong> comes from Riot's Data Dragon static data, checked into this repository so the build works without network access. Icons are loaded from Riot's public Data Dragon CDN by your browser; no champion art is hosted, copied or re-encoded here.</li></ul><h2>What the aggregation cannot know</h2><p>No aggregation has run for this build, so read this section as a limit of the method rather than as a caveat on numbers you can see: there are none.</p><p>Rank attribution can only ever be a snapshot, not a per-match fact. A match record does not carry the rank a player held in that match, so a bracket has to be built from players discovered at that rank in a recent window. A match is attributed to the bracket its players were found through, which means the bracket on this page is where those accounts were seen, not where every participant was at the time of every game. Treat bracket figures as approximate stratification rather than a precise rank filter.</p><p>Two more limits are worth stating plainly. Aggregates describe the matches in the crawl window, and the ranked landscape moves during a patch, so an early-patch reading and a late-patch reading of the same patch are different datasets. And because the aggregation only ever sees matches the pipeline was able to fetch, a champion that is rare in the crawled population can stay below the publication threshold for a whole patch.</p><h2>Source, freshness and provenance of this build</h2><p>Freshness here means the crawl window and the patch, not the moment you loaded the page: there is no live data on this site. Five values describe any snapshot, and each page prints the ones that apply to the numbers it shows.</p><ul><li><strong>Patch</strong> - the game version the matches were played on, because balance changes invalidate a reading across a patch boundary.</li><li><strong>Region</strong> - the server the matches were played on. Regional metas differ, so rates are not pooled across regions.</li><li><strong>Queue</strong> - which queue the matches came from. The pipeline covers ranked solo/duo (queue 420) and nothing else; queue 420 and 440 are different populations.</li><li><strong>Bracket</strong> - the rank band the cell is drawn from, with the attribution limitation described above.</li><li><strong>Generated at and source window</strong> - when the aggregate build ran, and which match timestamps it covered. A snapshot can be old in either sense, and the two are not the same fact.</li></ul><ul><li>Aggregate root read at build time: checked-in demo fixtures</li><li>Manifest declares its source as <code>demo</code> (demo: illustrative fixtures, not match statistics)</li><li>Patches in this build: 16.18, 16.17</li><li>Every page states whether it is real, preview or empty in a banner at the top, and the state is decided from the manifest alone rather than from a build flag a later change could forget to set.</li></ul><h2>Attribution and notices</h2><p>League of Legends and Riot Games are trademarks or registered trademarks of Riot Games, Inc.</p><p>This project is not endorsed by Riot Games and does not reflect the views or opinions of Riot Games or anyone officially involved in producing or managing Riot Games properties. Riot Games and all associated properties are trademarks or registered trademarks of Riot Games, Inc.</p><p>This site does not compute, store or display an MMR, ELO or any other skill rating, and does not offer a calculator for one. No personal data is sold, brokered or shared, and the pages you are reading were produced from aggregate counts: they contain no per-player record, name, account or match history.</p><p>Champion icons and item, rune and spell icons are requested by your browser directly from Riot's Data Dragon CDN. They are never vendored into this repository, re-encoded or re-served, and that CDN sees your IP address the same way any other image host would. See the <a href="/legal/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> for what your browser talks to and what this site's own logs contain.</p><p>The text is set in Inter, JetBrains Mono and Montserrat. All three are self-hosted from <code>/fonts/</code> under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, and no font request leaves this deployment: the licence notices ship next to the files as <a href="/fonts/OFL-Inter.txt">OFL-Inter.txt</a>,<a href="/fonts/OFL-JetBrainsMono.txt">OFL-JetBrainsMono.txt</a> and<a href="/fonts/OFL-Montserrat.txt">OFL-Montserrat.txt</a>.</p><p>Questions about these pages, about the data, or about your rights under the GDPR go to lolstats@erik-schuetze.dev. That mailbox is the contact route rather than a postal address, because the operator is a private individual.</p><p>Corrections are welcome and are treated as faults: if a number here is wrong, the honest outcome is a corrected snapshot with a new build record, not a quiet edit. Write to <a href="mailto:lolstats@erik-schuetze.dev">lolstats@erik-schuetze.dev</a>.</p><p><small>Effective 17 September 2026. Last updated 17 September 2026.</small></p><h2>Rules and policies</h2><ul><li><a href="/legal/terms">Terms of Service</a></li><li><a href="/legal/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> - this site serves static content and sets no tracking cookies</li><li><a href="/disclaimer">Disclaimer</a> - Riot's non-endorsement notice and the limits of these numbers</li></ul><p><strong>No match data has been ingested yet</strong>, so nothing on this site is a measurement of real games. That is the state of the project, not a fault in the pages: the layout, the labels and the sample-size notices are finished and waiting for the first snapshot.</p></article>